WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.019
[MUSIC]

00:00:14.999 --> 00:00:18.515
Confidence in communication is
critical to business success, and

00:00:18.515 --> 00:00:20.610
it's a really simple equation.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.860
Confidence equals competence.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:26.350
The more confident you come across, the
more competent people will see you as, and

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:28.400
the more credible they'll see you as.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.305
So we really need to work to
make sure that we're confident.

00:00:31.305 --> 00:00:36.089
[MUSIC]

00:00:36.089 --> 00:00:41.067
Many of us get very anxious in high-stakes
communication situations, be it giving

00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:47.000
a presentation, running a meeting, or even
having an important personal conversation.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.470
So we need to learn to manage our anxiety.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:51.930
And I use that word very carefully.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:53.690
We don't want to overcome it.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.900
Anxiety actually helps us,
it gives us focus, it gives us energy.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:00.760
But we want to manage it,
so it doesn't manage us.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.940
And when it comes to
managing that anxiety,

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.120
we need to take a two-pronged approach.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.560
We need to first focus on the symptoms,
and

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:12.030
what we show people in terms of our
anxiety, and second we need to work on

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:17.340
the sources, what actually causes that
anxiety So let me give you an example.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:21.550
Many people, when they get nervous, they
feel their heart rate beating very fast,

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:23.330
and they begin to perspire or blush.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:27.000
This happens to me, and
a great way to manage those symptoms is,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.450
first, to take some deep belly breaths.

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:32.100
In doing that,
you slow down your heart rate.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.240
In addition, you get yourself
breathing in a lower place,

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:38.710
which allows you to support your voice
pattern so you don't have a shaky,

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:41.770
quivering voice,
as many nervous people do.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:42.920
In terms of the blushing and

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.740
the sweating, that's because
you're actually getting hotter.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:47.230
When you speak and

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:50.740
are nervous about speaking,
your core body temperature goes up.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.130
Just like you have a fever.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:53.640
And a great way to manage that,

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.370
much like you do when you have a fever,
is to make yourself cooler.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:01.217
Now you're not going to hold a cold
compress on your forehead when you're

00:02:01.217 --> 00:02:02.498
giving a presentation [LAUGH] but

00:02:02.498 --> 00:02:04.739
you could hold something cold
in the palm of your hands.

00:02:04.739 --> 00:02:09.292
[MUSIC]

00:02:09.292 --> 00:02:13.280
We need to think about what
is driving us to be nervous.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.390
For many of my students and the people I
coach, it has to do with, for example,

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:20.610
the future consequences of
what they're trying to do.

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.750
Many of us when When we speak
in high stake situations,

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:27.150
we're worried about not achieving
our communication goal.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.470
If I'm an entrepeneur,
I might want funding or advice.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:32.990
If I'm one of my students,
I might want a good grade or

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:35.590
to perform well on my My assignment.

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:40.490
And because we're worried about the future
consequences we get very nervous.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:45.440
So a great way to manage that source is
to actually focus on the present moment,

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.930
be present while speaking.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:48.820
How do you do that?

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:51.840
Well before you start speaking
do something physical,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.260
take a walk around the building,
shake hands with people in the audience,

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:00.020
get yourself in the moment Athletes,
before they perform their sport,

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:03.420
they listen to a playlist or
a song to help them focus.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.680
Speakers can do the same thing.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.650
My secret to get present oriented
is I love saying tongue twisters.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.130
It sounds silly but

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:14.570
saying a tongue twister forces
you to be in the present moment.

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:16.380
And it also warms up your voice.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:20.580
Many nervous speakers,
they don't take the time to warm up.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:25.012
So if you want to be more
confident when you present,

00:03:25.012 --> 00:03:30.269
you need to focus both on the symptoms and
the sources to help.

00:03:30.269 --> 00:03:32.438
[MUSIC]

00:03:32.438 --> 00:03:36.130
We have to start by thinking about,
what is our communication goal?

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.610
And a communication goal has three parts,

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:42.320
it's what do you want your
audience to know when you're done?

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:45.590
How do you want them to feel,
you want them to feel excited,

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:48.200
you want to feel concerned or
just confident?

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.270
And what do you want them to do?

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:54.060
If you have that speaking goal, not only
will it direct everything that you're

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:58.700
saying, but at the end of a presentation,
even if you're cut off towards the end,

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.200
you should be able to
recite that speaking goal.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.300
And it's a nice way to wrap things up.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:07.590
So you could say something like The bottom
line is I want you to know that it's

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:11.570
critical to prepare for your communication
and be confident in delivering it.

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:15.140
I want you to feel really
empowered that you can do that and

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:18.170
give meaningful presentations and
communication.

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:22.415
And ultimately I want you
to put this into practice.

00:04:22.415 --> 00:04:25.255
[MUSIC]

00:04:25.255 --> 00:04:27.549
The single best way to be more concise and

00:04:27.549 --> 00:04:32.810
more directed to your audience is to have
clear structure in what you're saying.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:37.440
When we ramble, when we are putting our
thoughts together as we're speaking,

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:42.070
that's when we risk talking too much and
turning our audience off.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:44.790
It's much better to start
with a speaking goal.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:48.750
What is it I want my audience to
know Feel and do when I'm done, and

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:51.330
then put that information
into a structure.

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:56.790
My favorite structure is the what,
so what, now what structure.

00:04:56.790 --> 00:05:01.690
I explain what my point is,
I explained next why it's so important.

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:02.890
That's the so what.

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:05.880
Then the now what is what
I'd like you to do with it.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.740
Having a structure like that not only
helps me be more confident because I know

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:15.870
how I'm going to say what I want to
say but it keeps me very concise.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:21.739
Once I'm done I'm done,
there's no need to say more.

00:05:21.739 --> 00:05:24.029
[MUSIC]

00:05:24.029 --> 00:05:28.727
In terms of presentations, I am on
a personal mission to help people stop

00:05:28.727 --> 00:05:32.577
the most common way of starting
which is Hi, my name is, and

00:05:32.577 --> 00:05:35.390
today I'd like to tell you about.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:37.100
And the funny thing for me in that,

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:40.680
is most of the time when people start
that way, what's behind them but

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:44.190
a slide projecting their name and
a the title of their presentation.

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.230
It's a wasted opportunity.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:49.330
I would much rather people
start their presentations or

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:54.270
even there meetings in some kind
of interactive way, ask a question

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:59.600
Take a poll, give some startling
statistic, or perhaps tell a story.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:03.740
All of those are ways to pull your
audience in, I like the joke that

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:08.540
a good presentation starts like a James
Bond movie, not with all the action and

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:13.360
the violence, but it starts with something
that gets you attached right away.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:16.950
And then after that, you see the title and
the credits for the move.

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:19.300
Presentation should start that way, too.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:22.410
Start with something that draws your
audience in, and then step back and

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:25.970
say your name and
your relationship to the topic.

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:30.950
And in terms of ending, we know that
both primacy, that's what happens first,

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:34.170
and recency, that's what you hear last,
are really critical.

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:39.110
So when you end a presentation Or you end
a communication, make sure you sum up

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:43.970
your key ideas, but help your audience to
understand what they can do with it, and

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:46.150
why it's important that they do that.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:50.110
The most common way I hear presentations
end in the business world where I do my

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:54.588
consulting Is oop, somebody else
has got the room, we got to leave.

00:06:54.588 --> 00:06:59.341
Or in my classes, people just say,
I guess I'm out of time, thanks.

00:06:59.341 --> 00:07:03.670
Those are missed opportunities,
so really think about how to end

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:08.563
with something memorable and
something that summarizes your key ideas.

00:07:08.563 --> 00:07:10.415
[MUSIC]

00:07:10.415 --> 00:07:12.571
Non-verbal communication
is really important.

00:07:12.571 --> 00:07:15.784
People make assessments
of your credibility and

00:07:15.784 --> 00:07:19.388
form impressions within
milliseconds of seeing you.

00:07:19.388 --> 00:07:24.820
Some really simple rules around what to
do in terms of your non-verbal presence.

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:28.175
Fundamentally it's about being big,
balanced and still.

00:07:28.175 --> 00:07:32.449
And by big I mean you want to be upright
you don't want to make yourself smaller,

00:07:32.449 --> 00:07:36.595
when we're nervous and concerned we
make ourselves small and constrict,

00:07:36.595 --> 00:07:37.514
we want to be big.

00:07:37.514 --> 00:07:41.921
We want to be balanced, meaning our head
is straight our shoulders are squared,

00:07:41.921 --> 00:07:44.158
we're not leaning we're not resting.

00:07:44.158 --> 00:07:46.170
Additionally, we want to be still.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:50.120
When we get nervous we fidget,
our hands move, our legs shake.

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:52.682
We want to make sure
that we seem composed.

00:07:52.682 --> 00:07:57.292
So the biggest thing to make a very
effective, quick impression in terms of

00:07:57.292 --> 00:08:00.610
your confidence is to be big,
balanced, and still.

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:03.972
When it comes to your hands,
you want them to be ecpansive.

00:08:03.972 --> 00:08:06.796
That is you want them to
go beyond your shoulders.

00:08:06.796 --> 00:08:10.295
A lot of people carry their hands
up tight and in front of them.

00:08:10.295 --> 00:08:12.180
It's a very defensive posture.

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:14.534
So we wanto to make
sure that we're out and

00:08:14.534 --> 00:08:17.940
that when we're not gesturing
our hands are still in calm.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:22.798
Those are by far the most important
non verbal postures we can take

00:08:22.798 --> 00:08:24.574
to convey confidence.

00:08:24.574 --> 00:08:28.274
[MUSIC]

00:08:28.274 --> 00:08:31.448
Making mistakes while speaking isn't
normal and natural we're human beings.

00:08:31.448 --> 00:08:36.007
One thing to do is not call
attention to it, not focus on it,

00:08:36.007 --> 00:08:40.230
sometimes when,
when I make a mistake I'll just say.

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:43.334
I'm just so excited about this topic,
I get ahead of myself.

00:08:43.334 --> 00:08:44.946
Let's go back and start again.

00:08:44.946 --> 00:08:48.588
Othertimes, what I'll do,
is I'll just ask the audience a question.

00:08:48.588 --> 00:08:52.405
So if I find myself floundering, or
if I made a mistake, I'll stop and say,

00:08:52.405 --> 00:08:56.260
let's take a moment to reflect on
the value of what we've discussed so far.

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:57.724
What does this mean for you?

00:08:57.724 --> 00:08:59.310
And while they're thinking or

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:02.482
perhaps I'll even have them turn
to the person next to them and

00:09:02.482 --> 00:09:06.752
exchange their answers, that gives me time
to reset and get myself back on course.

00:09:06.752 --> 00:09:10.473
There are times when people in your
audience can be challenging for you.

00:09:10.473 --> 00:09:14.804
It could be they're asking a difficult
question, perhaps they have a disagreement

00:09:14.804 --> 00:09:18.610
with something you've said, or
they just want to air their point of view.

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:20.516
That can be very challenging.

00:09:20.516 --> 00:09:24.988
The difficulty for you is not only
handling that person in a respectful,

00:09:24.988 --> 00:09:29.313
polite way, but you also have to be
very cognizant of your audience's

00:09:29.313 --> 00:09:31.512
perception of how you manage that.

00:09:31.512 --> 00:09:35.752
So So I like to say I have two kids, and
they like to play lots of video games.

00:09:35.752 --> 00:09:40.225
And in their video games, their characters
have life points above their head.

00:09:40.225 --> 00:09:43.428
And as they do more poorly,
they lose those life points.

00:09:43.428 --> 00:09:46.832
As a speaker, you have credibility
points above your head.

00:09:46.832 --> 00:09:50.836
And if you handle a situation like
that poorly, you lose credibility.

00:09:50.836 --> 00:09:54.104
To me, the most valuable tool
to help deal with hecklers or

00:09:54.104 --> 00:09:56.360
challenging people is paraphrasing.

00:09:56.360 --> 00:10:01.341
Listen to what they're saying for a little
bit, then paraphrase what they've said and

00:10:01.341 --> 00:10:01.878
move on.

00:10:01.878 --> 00:10:06.387
So it might be something like, I clearly
hear you have some passion on this topic

00:10:06.387 --> 00:10:10.491
and I appreciate your feedback, but
what I'd like to do is address, and

00:10:10.491 --> 00:10:15.210
what I've done is I've acknowledged
their Energy, perhaps their emotion, and

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:18.210
then I've redirected to
the next place I'm going.

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:21.824
I've been known in meetings where
somebody gets up in my face,

00:10:21.824 --> 00:10:26.662
I've been known to say, thank you for
sharing, clearly you're very passionate.

00:10:26.662 --> 00:10:30.617
I'm curious to know what somebody
on this side of room thinks about,

00:10:30.617 --> 00:10:32.526
